ukrainians are hiding in basements and  shelters and others are fleeing trying  to avoid the fighting and it's creating  a massive humanitarian crisis all  throughout that region this morning the  u.n said almost 900 000 people have fled  to neighboring countries that does not  even include internally displaced people  inside ukraine  crowds at some border crossings are so  large that people are now waiting up to  five days to get through christina  raffini is in poland for us talking to  refugees who tell harrowing stories  about their escape out of the war zone  in ukraine it's like hell  so  we're running  running west away from the violence  across crowded borders  here in poland the journey can take up  to 60 hours much of it by foot  amazing  goodbye this sister and brother walked  alone overnight to get to the medica  crossing their father dropped them off  as close as he could get 11 miles from  the border  but on the other side help is waiting at  a receiving center a few miles away  muhammad and his family loaded into a  van driven by volunteers all the way  from germany we decided to move as far  as we can because it wasn't safe at all  at the crowded train stations in ukraine  he says police tried to separate him  from his family saying women and  children first the priority for women  and the children okay but if there is  family should all family should be  together but after some of the worst  days of their lives they made it  together  mother-in-law natalia says thank you to  all the kind people who helped them  and  may there be peace  now ukrainian men aren't allowed to  leave muhammad has an egyptian passport  though we talked to several african  medical students at this train station  behind me yesterday all women who told  us the men in their party were prevented  from boarding trains out of ukraine even  though they're also foreign passport  holders they were told women and  children first  tony christina raffini in poland for us  christina thank you very much
 

